<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Antrim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County Antrim is a county in the north-east of Northern Ireland, between Lough Neagh and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>, and includes much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>. Its coast, with the basalt columns of the Giant's Causeway, lies only some 20 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was home to the Dál nAraidi and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, whose kings extended their power across the sea into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-antrim.jpg" alt="County Antrim" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest; Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa); derivative work by Rannpháirtí anaithnid; derivative work by Mabuska · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Island_of_Ireland_location_map_Antrim.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>County Antrim is one of the six counties of Northern Ireland, in the historic province of Ulster, and one of the thirty-two traditional counties of Ireland. It is named after the town of Antrim, from the Irish Aontroim, 'lone ridge', and covers 3,086 square kilometres next to the north-east shore of Lough Neagh. A large part of the county is hilly, especially in the east. On the northern shore the hills end in abrupt cliffs, notably the basalt columns at Fair Head and the Giant's Causeway. The fertile lowlands lie in the valleys of the Bann and the Lagan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> hunter-gatherers, and the Mount Sandel site at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coleraine">Coleraine</a>, beside the Bann, shows human habitation around 7000 BCE. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> southern County Antrim belonged to the Kingdom of Ulidia, and the north was part of Dál Riada, which stretched across the sea into what is now western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The Dál nAraide held lower County Antrim. Between the 8th and 11th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> raided the coast.</p><p>In the late 12th century Antrim became part of the Earldom of Ulster, conquered by Anglo-Norman invaders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-bruce">Edward Bruce</a>'s campaign of 1315 brought a revival of Gaelic power and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrickfergus">Carrickfergus</a> as the only significant English stronghold. In the 15th century the MacDonnells, a gallowglass family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, became the most powerful in the Glens, and in the 16th century many Scots settled in the county. In 1588 the Spanish ship La Girona was wrecked off the Giant's Causeway with the loss of nearly 1,300 lives. In 1689, in the Williamite War, Antrim was a centre of Protestant resistance to the Catholic James II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>, the capital of Northern Ireland, lies mostly in County Antrim, with the rest in County Down. Antrim was administered by Antrim County Council from 1899 until 1973, and its traditional county town is Antrim. Carrickfergus Castle, dating from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Norman invasion of Ireland</a>, is one of the best preserved medieval structures in Ireland, and Dunluce Castle stands on a dramatic rocky outcrop. The Giant's Causeway is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Slemish, near Ballymena, is traditionally the place where Saint Patrick spent his years as a slave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County Antrim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dál Riata was a Gaelic kingdom that straddled the North Channel, with lands in the north-east of what is now <b>County Antrim</b> and, across the sea, in Argyll and the islands of the Inner Hebrides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 6th century they held south and central <b>County Antrim</b>, with their royal centre in Mag Line near the town of Antrim, and they competed with the Dal Fiatach for the kingship of the Kingdom of Ulaid.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-naraidi">Dal nAraidi</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 lists the Darini in north-east Ireland, inland or on the coast between the Robogdii and the Voluntii, in what is now <b>County Antrim</b> or north Down.</blockquote><a href="/state/darini">Darini</a></li><li><blockquote>The earls held coastal <b>Antrim</b> and Down with castles, towns such as Carrickfergus and Coleraine, and manors worked by English settlers.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-ulster">Earldom of Ulster</a></li><li><blockquote>The peninsula lies only about 20 kilometres from <b>Antrim</b>, and contact with Ireland was constant across the North Channel.</blockquote><a href="/state/epidii">Epidii</a></li><li><blockquote>In historic times the kingdom was an over-kingship shared between the Dal Fiatach of Down and the Dal nAraidi of <b>Antrim</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Kingdom of Ulaid</a></li></ul></section>
